WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:07.823
A synapse must know that its input fired, and that its own cell is active.

2
00:00:08.643 --> 00:00:09.963
What molecule could read both?

3
00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:14.267
Look at one synapse, from CA3 onto CA1.

4
00:00:15.047 --> 00:00:19.187
The terminal releases glutamate onto a spine, a small bud on the dendrite.

5
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:23.407
Two kinds of ionotropic glutamate receptor sit there.

6
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:27.650
AMPA receptors need only glutamate to open.

7
00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:30.710
They carry most of the ordinary EPSP.

8
00:00:31.683 --> 00:00:34.423
NMDA receptors also bind glutamate.

9
00:00:35.383 --> 00:00:39.783
They need a co-agonist too, glycine or D-serine, which is normally present.

10
00:00:40.817 --> 00:00:45.197
But at rest, a magnesium ion from outside sits in their pore and blocks it.

11
00:00:46.117 --> 00:00:49.857
Once the block clears, they pass sodium, potassium and calcium.

12
00:00:50.933 --> 00:00:52.133
What clears the block?

13
00:00:53.373 --> 00:00:58.033
Depolarization. Magnesium is positive, held in by the negative inside.

14
00:00:58.973 --> 00:01:01.533
Make the inside less negative, and it is pushed out.

15
00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:04.627
Where does the depolarization come from?

16
00:01:05.727 --> 00:01:08.187
Recall the balance point from the action potential lesson.

17
00:01:08.967 --> 00:01:10.327
It works like a tug of war.

18
00:01:11.317 --> 00:01:16.577
Take an isolated compartment, with fixed ohmic conductances, and no injected or

19
00:01:16.577 --> 00:01:20.737
axial current. Its voltage relaxes toward a weighted average.

20
00:01:21.637 --> 00:01:25.877
Each current's reversal potential counts in proportion to its conductance.

21
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.900
AMPA current reverses near 0 mV.

22
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:34.800
When many receptors open at once, they pull the local balance point up from near

23
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:35.800
minus −70

24
00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:41.730
A spike fired by the cell and spreading back into the dendrite also depolarizes the

25
00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:42.730
spine.

26
00:01:43.083 --> 00:01:46.763
So the spine voltage reports what the postsynaptic cell is doing.

27
00:01:47.563 --> 00:01:49.823
And the magnesium block reads that voltage.

28
00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:54.103
A spike is one source of depolarization, not the only one.

29
00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:58.527
Now put the signals together, as a product of three factors.

30
00:01:59.427 --> 00:02:01.047
First, glutamate must be bound.

31
00:02:01.887 --> 00:02:04.087
Second, depolarization clears the block.

32
00:02:05.047 --> 00:02:07.067
Third, calcium still needs an inward push.

33
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:10.210
The active terminal supplies glutamate.

34
00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:15.390
The other two factors are set by the voltage of the postsynaptic membrane.

35
00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:21.070
With no glutamate, no calcium enters through these receptors, at any voltage.

36
00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:25.230
That makes the NMDA receptor a coincidence detector.

37
00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:31.120
Near rest, with glutamate bound, about one receptor in twenty is unblocked.

38
00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:34.420
Depolarize the spine, and about one in three is.

39
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.040
Glutamate arrives at a spine sitting near rest.

40
00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:40.160
Does any calcium get in?

41
00:02:46.083 --> 00:02:49.323
A little. The gate is graded, not a switch.

42
00:02:50.243 --> 00:02:54.923
Even at rest a few receptors are unblocked, so glutamate alone lets in a trickle.

43
00:02:55.967 --> 00:02:58.827
more depolarization is not always better.

44
00:02:59.667 --> 00:03:04.707
In this model, with normal magnesium, calcium entry through the receptor peaks near

45
00:03:04.707 --> 00:03:07.007
minus −20 mV, then falls.

46
00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:09.540
That is the third factor.

47
00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.320
Depolarization weakens the inward push on calcium.

48
00:03:14.333 --> 00:03:20.173
So for the same receptor, minus −30 instead of minus −70 speeds calcium entry only

49
00:03:20.173 --> 00:03:23.673
about four times. Where does the big difference come from?

50
00:03:24.617 --> 00:03:25.997
Mainly from summation.

51
00:03:26.877 --> 00:03:31.017
During a fast train, glutamate keeps arriving faster than the receptors close.

52
00:03:31.897 --> 00:03:33.277
And the depolarization builds.

53
00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:38.860
The receptors stay activated while the responses summate, and the calcium in the

54
00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:40.080
spine climbs steeply.

55
00:03:41.033 --> 00:03:47.853
The takeaway: at normal magnesium levels, an NMDA receptor lets in much calcium only

56
00:03:47.853 --> 00:03:50.353
when glutamate and depolarization coincide.
